@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2731 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [3691 x i8] }> <{ [3691 x i8] c"# `lang_items`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: None.\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0AThe `rustc` compiler has certain pluggable operations, that is,\0Afunctionality that isn't hard-coded into the language, but is\0Aimplemented in libraries, with a special marker to tell the compiler\0Ait exists. The marker is the attribute `#[lang = \22...\22]` and there are\0Avarious different values of `...`, i.e. various different 'lang\0Aitems'. Most of them can only be defined once.\0A\0ALang items are loaded lazily by the compiler; e.g. if one never uses `Box`\0Athen there is no need to define a function for `exchange_malloc`.\0A`rustc` will emit an error when an item is needed but not found in the current\0Acrate or any that it depends on.\0A\0ASome features provided by lang items:\0A\0A- overloadable operators via traits: the traits corresponding to the\0A  `==`, `<`, dereferencing (`*`) and `+` (etc.) operators are all\0A  marked with lang items; those specific four are `eq`, `partial_ord`,\0A  `deref`/`deref_mut`, and `add` respectively.\0A- panicking: the `panic` and `panic_impl` lang items, among others.\0A- stack unwinding: the lang item `eh_personality` is a function used by the\0A  failure mechanisms of the compiler. This is often mapped to GCC's personality\0A  function (see the [`std` implementation][personality] for more information),\0A  but programs which don't trigger a panic can be assured that this function is\0A  never called. Additionally, a `eh_catch_typeinfo` static is needed for certain\0A  targets which implement Rust panics on top of C++ exceptions.\0A- the traits in `core::marker` used to indicate types of\0A  various kinds; e.g. lang items `sized`, `sync` and `copy`.\0A- memory allocation, see below.\0A\0AMost lang items are defined by `core`, but if you're trying to build\0Aan executable without the `std` crate, you might run into the need\0Afor lang item definitions.\0A\0A[personality]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/master/library/std/src/sys/personality/gcc.rs\0A\0A## Example: Implementing a `Box`\0A\0A`Box` pointers require two lang items: one for the type itself and one for\0Aallocation. A freestanding program that uses the `Box` sugar for dynamic\0Aallocations via `malloc` and `free`:\0A\0A```rust,ignore (libc-is-finicky)\0A#![feature(lang_items, start, core_intrinsics, rustc_private, panic_unwind, rustc_attrs)]\0A#![allow(internal_features)]\0A#![no_std]\0A\0Aextern crate libc;\0Aextern crate unwind;\0A\0Ause core::ffi::c_void;\0Ause core::intrinsics;\0Ause core::panic::PanicInfo;\0Ause core::ptr::NonNull;\0A\0Apub struct Global; // the global allocator\0Astruct Unique<T>(NonNull<T>);\0A\0A#[lang = \22owned_box\22]\0Apub struct Box<T, A = Global>(Unique<T>, A);\0A\0Aimpl<T> Box<T> {\0A    pub fn new(x: T) -> Self {\0A        #[rustc_box]\0A        Box::new(x)\0A    }\0A}\0A\0Aimpl<T, A> Drop for Box<T, A> {\0A    fn drop(&mut self) {\0A        unsafe {\0A            libc::free(self.0.0.as_ptr() as *mut c_void);\0A        }\0A    }\0A}\0A\0A#[lang = \22exchange_malloc\22]\0Aunsafe fn allocate(size: usize, _align: usize) -> *mut u8 {\0A    let p = libc::malloc(size) as *mut u8;\0A\0A    // Check if `malloc` failed:\0A    if p.is_null() {\0A        intrinsics::abort();\0A    }\0A\0A    p\0A}\0A\0A#[start]\0Afn main(_argc: isize, _argv: *const *const u8) -> isize {\0A    let _x = Box::new(1);\0A\0A    0\0A}\0A\0A#[lang = \22eh_personality\22]\0Afn rust_eh_personality() {}\0A\0A#[panic_handler]\0Afn panic_handler(_info: &PanicInfo) -> ! { intrinsics::abort() }\0A```\0A\0ANote the use of `abort`: the `exchange_malloc` lang item is assumed to\0Areturn a valid pointer, and so needs to do the check internally.\0A\0A## List of all language items\0A\0AAn up-to-date list of all language items can be found [here] in the compiler code.\0A\0A[here]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/master/compiler/rustc_hir/src/lang_items.rs\0A" }>, align 1
